1
00:00:00,033 --> 00:00:10,033
Here is an equation, and its curve. We want its rational solutions: points where both coordinates are fractions.

2
00:00:10,033 --> 00:00:15,667
Some curves have none. Some have a handful. Some have infinitely many.

3
00:00:15,667 --> 00:00:21,167
Is there a way to tell which, without searching forever?

4
00:00:21,167 --> 00:00:27,367
Two mathematicians guessed one in the nineteen sixties, from a computer experiment.

5
00:00:27,367 --> 00:00:30,333
It became a Millennium Prize problem.

6
00:00:30,333 --> 00:00:39,667
In October 2026, OpenAI claimed to prove its strongest form for a large class of curves.

7
00:00:45,933 --> 00:00:51,967
Rational points on such a curve can be added. Here is how.

8
00:00:51,967 --> 00:00:54,467
Draw the line through two of them.

9
00:00:54,467 --> 00:01:05,300
It meets the curve at exactly one more point, and that point is rational too, because the algebra only uses adding and dividing.

10
00:01:05,300 --> 00:01:10,167
Reflect it across the curve's line of symmetry, and you get their sum.

11
00:01:10,167 --> 00:01:17,767
This rule turns the rational points into a group, like the whole numbers under addition.

12
00:01:17,767 --> 00:01:27,500
Adding a point to itself, again and again, can produce infinitely many points. Watch the fractions grow.

13
00:01:27,500 --> 00:01:35,600
In 1922, Mordell proved that all the rational points are built from a finite set of starting points.

14
00:01:35,600 --> 00:01:39,667
The number of independent starting points is the rank.

15
00:01:39,667 --> 00:01:47,533
On this curve every point is a multiple of one, so its rank is one.

16
00:01:47,533 --> 00:01:54,633
A rank zero curve has only finitely many rational points. Higher rank means more room.

17
00:01:54,633 --> 00:02:00,800
But nobody knows a procedure that always computes the rank.

18
00:02:00,800 --> 00:02:10,133
So: rational points can be added, and the rank counts the independent ones.

19
00:02:14,366 --> 00:02:22,200
Instead of searching for rational points, Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer counted solutions modulo primes.

20
00:02:22,200 --> 00:02:30,933
Work with remainders after dividing by seven, and count the pairs that satisfy the equation.

21
00:02:30,933 --> 00:02:38,966
On average, the count is about the prime itself. But a curve with many rational points should have slightly more,

22
00:02:38,966 --> 00:02:46,833
because every rational point gives a solution modulo almost every prime.

23
00:02:46,833 --> 00:02:53,600
So they multiplied the ratios, count over p, for all primes up to some limit.

24
00:02:53,600 --> 00:02:59,700
For a curve of rank zero, the product stayed roughly level.

25
00:02:59,700 --> 00:03:05,966
For rank one it grew like the logarithm of the limit. For rank two, like its square.

26
00:03:05,966 --> 00:03:10,800
The rank was showing up as the power of the growth.

27
00:03:10,800 --> 00:03:21,033
We recomputed their experiment for primes up to a hundred thousand. The four curves separate cleanly, by rank.

28
00:03:21,033 --> 00:03:26,100
These counts are packaged into one function, the curve's L-function.

29
00:03:26,100 --> 00:03:34,033
Its behaviour at one point, s equals one, mirrors that growth.

30
00:03:34,033 --> 00:03:42,100
The conjecture says: the rank of the curve equals the number of times its L-function vanishes at s equals one.

31
00:03:42,100 --> 00:03:47,200
That number is called the analytic rank.

32
00:03:47,200 --> 00:03:57,066
So: count solutions modulo primes, and the L-function at one should reveal the rank.

33
00:04:01,300 --> 00:04:07,834
They went further, and predicted exactly how big the L-function is when it starts:

34
00:04:07,834 --> 00:04:13,634
an exact formula for its first nonzero Taylor coefficient.

35
00:04:13,634 --> 00:04:19,834
On the left: the L-function's first nonzero coefficient.

36
00:04:19,834 --> 00:04:30,434
On the right, a list of measurements of the same curve: its size as a real shape, and how spread out its rational points are.

37
00:04:30,434 --> 00:04:40,034
Local factors at the primes where the curve goes bad, and the points that return to the start when added to themselves.

38
00:04:40,034 --> 00:04:46,300
And one mysterious piece: the Tate–Shafarevich group, often called Sha.

39
00:04:46,300 --> 00:04:52,067
It counts near-misses: curves that have solutions modulo every prime power,

40
00:04:52,067 --> 00:04:58,300
and over the real numbers, but still no rational solution.

41
00:04:58,300 --> 00:05:07,100
Sha is conjectured to be finite. For most curves, nobody can prove even that.

42
00:05:07,100 --> 00:05:18,334
So the full prediction is an exact formula: one L-value, equal to a product of the curve's measurements.

43
00:05:22,566 --> 00:05:28,533
The rank prediction is known only when the analytic rank is zero or one.

44
00:05:28,533 --> 00:05:34,433
In the nineteen eighties, Gross and Zagier built special points,

45
00:05:34,433 --> 00:05:41,000
called Heegner points, whose size is given by the L-function's slope.

46
00:05:41,000 --> 00:05:48,866
Kolyvagin then showed that in analytic rank zero or one, the rank matches and Sha is finite.

47
00:05:48,866 --> 00:05:58,833
And since 2001, every such curve is known to be modular, so this applies to all of them.

48
00:05:58,833 --> 00:06:01,500
The exact formula was harder.

49
00:06:01,500 --> 00:06:11,266
Proving it means matching the two sides prime by prime: for every prime, the same number of factors on each side.

50
00:06:11,266 --> 00:06:15,400
Earlier work proved it at many primes for many curves.

51
00:06:15,400 --> 00:06:20,300
But each result needed extra conditions on the curve or the prime.

52
00:06:20,300 --> 00:06:27,666
No proof covered every prime for every curve of rank zero or one.

53
00:06:27,666 --> 00:06:38,133
So in rank zero and one, the rank was known, but the exact formula only in part.

54
00:06:42,366 --> 00:06:47,000
To state the claim we need one more object, the Selmer group.

55
00:06:47,000 --> 00:06:55,266
It collects everything that looks like a rational point when you check the curve modulo powers of a prime.

56
00:06:55,266 --> 00:07:01,466
It contains the true rational points, so its size gives an upper bound on the rank.

57
00:07:01,466 --> 00:07:07,433
And unlike the rank, it can often be computed.

58
00:07:07,433 --> 00:07:13,800
Here is the claim. If this upper bound is zero or one, at even a single prime,

59
00:07:13,800 --> 00:07:23,066
then the rank equals it, Sha is finite, and the exact formula holds, at every prime.

60
00:07:23,066 --> 00:07:28,533
No extra conditions on the curve are needed.

61
00:07:28,533 --> 00:07:40,100
So for a curve whose Selmer bound is zero or one, everything Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer predicted would be true.

62
00:07:44,333 --> 00:07:47,267
The paper is clear about what is new.

63
00:07:47,267 --> 00:07:55,767
It takes four inputs: three companion papers from OpenAI, and one published theorem.

64
00:07:55,767 --> 00:08:03,667
The first companion gives the rank and the finiteness of Sha. The second gives the formula at the prime two.

65
00:08:03,667 --> 00:08:09,000
The new step is every other prime.

66
00:08:09,000 --> 00:08:20,033
For each odd prime p, write X for the number of factors of p by which the formula is off. The goal is to show X is zero.

67
00:08:20,033 --> 00:08:26,667
First, a one-sided inequality: X is at least zero, for each curve.

68
00:08:26,667 --> 00:08:32,267
The L-value never has fewer factors of p than predicted.

69
00:08:32,267 --> 00:08:39,333
Next, a sister curve: a twist of the original, chosen to have the opposite analytic rank.

70
00:08:39,333 --> 00:08:43,733
The same inequality applies to it.

71
00:08:43,733 --> 00:08:51,500
Then the hard part: a pair identity. The two errors add up to exactly zero.

72
00:08:51,500 --> 00:09:02,467
Two numbers that are each at least zero, and add up to zero, must both be zero. So the formula is exact at p.

73
00:09:02,467 --> 00:09:09,933
The pair identity combines the curve and its twist over a new number system, where a Heegner point lives.

74
00:09:09,933 --> 00:09:16,533
The paper builds two power series, one from L-values and one from Galois cohomology,

75
00:09:16,533 --> 00:09:24,300
and shows their ratio is a unit: neither has a factor the other lacks.

76
00:09:24,300 --> 00:09:30,933
That takes four stages: the ratio is integral, it has no factor of p,

77
00:09:30,933 --> 00:09:40,633
theta series rule out other common factors, and extra variables push away whatever is left.

78
00:09:40,633 --> 00:09:49,267
Put together with the prime two, the ratio of the two sides is a positive fraction with no prime factors at all.

79
00:09:49,267 --> 00:09:55,500
So it equals one, and the formula holds exactly.

80
00:09:55,500 --> 00:10:06,067
So, at each prime: at least zero, twice, adding up to zero. Exactly zero.

81
00:10:10,300 --> 00:10:15,167
A companion paper claims that, for any elliptic curve,

82
00:10:15,167 --> 00:10:22,834
half of its twists have analytic rank zero and half have rank one, with the rest negligible.

83
00:10:22,834 --> 00:10:33,634
Combined with this paper, the full Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer formula would hold for almost all twists of every curve.

84
00:10:33,634 --> 00:10:40,800
Another companion applies the result to the curves x cubed plus y cubed equals a prime.

85
00:10:40,800 --> 00:10:51,967
It concludes that every prime leaving remainder four, seven or eight on division by nine is a sum of two rational cubes.

86
00:10:51,967 --> 00:10:55,300
Some need surprisingly large fractions.

87
00:10:55,300 --> 00:11:06,734
Fifty-three is minus eighteen nineteen over two seventeen, cubed, plus eighteen seventy-two over two seventeen, cubed.

88
00:11:06,734 --> 00:11:18,800
So: the full formula for almost all twists, and a classical question about cubes, if it all holds up.

89
00:11:23,033 --> 00:11:29,867
The theorem stands on this ninety-four page paper, plus three companions of eighty-two,

90
00:11:29,867 --> 00:11:39,067
a hundred and sixty-four, and a hundred and thirty pages. All of them have to be right.

91
00:11:39,067 --> 00:11:48,933
Unlike OpenAI's Navier–Stokes and quasi-Riemann papers, there is no computer-checked proof in Lean.

92
00:11:48,933 --> 00:12:00,067
It was first posted on the third of October 2026. The seventh of October revision only updates references.

93
00:12:00,067 --> 00:12:09,933
As of the fourth of November, we found no public assessment by an expert, and no paper responding to it.

94
00:12:09,933 --> 00:12:20,133
So: four unreviewed papers, no formal proof, and no expert verdict yet.

95
00:12:24,366 --> 00:12:30,166
It does not solve the Millennium problem. That asks for every elliptic curve.

96
00:12:30,166 --> 00:12:41,833
Curves of rank two or more, like this one, are untouched. For them, nobody can prove even that Sha is finite.

97
00:12:41,833 --> 00:12:51,500
The pattern Birch and Swinnerton-Dyer saw in their experiment is still unproved for most of these curves.

98
00:12:51,500 --> 00:13:03,600
But for curves with at most one independent point, their prediction may now be complete, down to every last prime.
